window.__labLoaded({"id":"5.1","title":"Confidence Intervals","goal":"Build a 95% z-interval for a mean when sigma is known, then simulate thousands of intervals to see about 95% catch the true mean.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"The z-interval by formula","text":"The lesson's exam: sigma = 12 is known, n = 40 and the sample mean is 78.5. Three steps: standard error, margin of error (z* times SE), then the interval.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nsigma, n, xbar = 12, 40, 78.5\nz = stats.norm.ppf(0.975)\nse = sigma / np.sqrt(n)\nme = z * se\n\nprint(f'z* = {z:.3f}, SE = {se:.3f}, margin = {me:.2f}')\nprint(f'95% CI = ({xbar - me:.2f}, {xbar + me:.2f})')","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Confidence level changes the width","text":"Being more confident means casting a wider net. We repeat the calculation for 90%, 95% and 99%, using the matching z*.","code":"for level in (0.90, 0.95, 0.99):\n    z = stats.norm.ppf(1 - (1 - level) / 2)\n    me = z * se\n    print(f'{level:.0%}: z* = {z:.3f}, margin = {me:.2f}, ({xbar - me:.2f}, {xbar + me:.2f})')","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Sample size changes the width","text":"At 95%, quadrupling n halves the margin of error, the 1 over square root of n rule again.","code":"for n_try in (10, 40, 160, 640):\n    margin = 1.96 * sigma / np.sqrt(n_try)\n    print(f'n = {n_try:3d}: margin = {margin:.2f}')","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Does it really catch the truth?","text":"Make the truth known: a normal population with mean 78.5 and SD 12. Draw 10,000 samples of 40, build a 95% interval from each, and count how many contain 78.5.","code":"rng = np.random.default_rng(1)\ntrue_mean = 78.5\nmeans = rng.normal(true_mean, sigma, size=(10_000, 40)).mean(axis=1)\nme = 1.96 * sigma / np.sqrt(40)\ncaught = (means - me <= true_mean) & (true_mean <= means + me)\n\nprint(f'intervals that caught the true mean: {caught.mean():.1%}')\nprint('intervals that missed:', (~caught).sum(), 'of 10000')","out":"","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"sigma <- 12; n <- 40; xbar <- 78.5\nz <- qnorm(0.975)\nme <- z * sigma / sqrt(n)\nc(z, me, xbar - me, xbar + me)\nset.seed(1)\nmeans <- replicate(10000, mean(rnorm(40, 78.5, 12)))\nmean(abs(means - 78.5) <= me)","expect":"z = 1.960, margin = 3.72, interval 74.78 to 82.22; coverage close to 0.95 (Python: 95.0%)","note":"Base R has no z-interval command, so we use qnorm and the formula. The coverage figure is simulated, so it is close to 95% but not identical to Python's."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (use IDF.NORMAL in the function list)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / sigma n xbar.\nBEGIN DATA\n12 40 78.5\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE z = IDF.NORMAL(0.975, 0, 1).\nCOMPUTE se = sigma / SQRT(n).\nCOMPUTE me = z * se.\nCOMPUTE lower = xbar - me.\nCOMPUTE upper = xbar + me.\nLIST VARIABLES=z se me lower upper.","note":"SPSS has no menu for a known-sigma interval, so we compute it from the formula; IDF.NORMAL(0.975, 0, 1) gives z* = 1.960. The coverage simulation is not repeated here."},"stata":{"code":"scalar z = invnormal(0.975)\nscalar se = 12 / sqrt(40)\nscalar me = z * se\ndisplay \"z* = \" z\ndisplay \"SE = \" se\ndisplay \"margin = \" me\ndisplay \"95% CI = (\" (78.5 - me) \", \" (78.5 + me) \")\"","note":"Stata's own interval commands assume sigma is unknown (t-based), so the known-sigma interval is built from invnormal() and the formula. It matches Python's 74.78 to 82.22."},"try":["Change the confidence level to 99% in the first block and predict whether the interval gets wider or narrower.","Change sigma to 6 and predict what happens to the margin of error.","In the simulation, use a z* of 1.645 (a 90% interval) in place of 1.96. What share of intervals should catch the truth now?"]});
